<Project>

  <!--
    Undo the file copying in TraceEvent's props, all of which is either unused or already done.

    msdia140.dll and KernelTraceControl.dll are ten megabytes across x86, amd64 and arm64, serving
    symbol resolution and kernel-session merging. The frame counter reads event headers and decodes
    no payload, so nothing ever loads them.

    Dia2Lib and TraceReloggerLib are COM interop the props copies out of lib/netstandard2.0 - the
    same folder NuGet already resolves them from as references. Keeping both copies hands crossgen
    the same assembly twice under one simple name and ReadyToRun fails the publish outright:
    "Multiple input files matching same simple name". They still ship, by the reference path they
    were always going to arrive on.

    This lives here rather than in SysTuneX.Core, which is where the package is referenced, because
    a transitive PackageReference propagates to every project downstream - so the app re-imports
    the same props and re-adds every item Core just removed. Doing it once for the whole tree is
    both shorter and the only version that actually holds.

    It has to be a .targets: Directory.Build.props is imported before NuGet's generated props, so
    at that point there would be nothing to remove yet.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'@(None)' != ''">
    <None Remove="@(None->WithMetadataValue('Filename','msdia140'))" />
    <None Remove="@(None->WithMetadataValue('Filename','KernelTraceControl'))" />
    <None Remove="@(None->WithMetadataValue('Filename','KernelTraceControl.Win61'))" />
    <None Remove="@(None->WithMetadataValue('Filename','Dia2Lib'))" />
    <None Remove="@(None->WithMetadataValue('Filename','TraceReloggerLib'))" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
